A1","created":"2026-10-14T12:44:18","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4527147919,"indicator":"3315ba12d148793dc570e19b419803365cfd51c9","type":"FileHash-SHA1","created":"2026-10-14T12:44:18","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4527147920,"indicator":"a00c7f45cb7b46a3fd8d15558e361a81d6363f13","type":"FileHash-SHA1","created":"2026-10-14T12:44:18","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4527147921,"indicator":"e70e1cf7d16bcad47e7dbe95fbcc65101e315445","type":"FileHash-SHA1","created":"2026-10-14T12:44:18","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4527147922,"indicator":"ec89131f976437a29f4e3b008569291681254109","type":"FileHash-SHA1","created":"2026-10-14T12:44:18","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4527147923,"indicator":"f77e32f96aaf9b9d10b09d7212bb4bb0dbd90c4e","type":"FileHash-SHA1","created":"2026-10-14T12:44:18","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null}],"tags":["proxy backdoor","cve-2025-34035","cve-2022-36553","iot exploitation","edge device targeting","botnet","cve-2024-23625","clingstun","stun abuse","mirai","device persistence","nat traversal"],"targeted_countries":[],"malware_families":["ClingSTUN","Mirai"],"attack_ids":[],"references":["https:\/\/blog.polyswarm.io\/clingstun-turns-vulnerable-edge-devices-into-attacker-infrastructure?"],"industries":[],"extract_source":[],"more_indicators":false},{"id":"6af6dd7f5c46ed2857e7d678","name":"Stolen webcam clips of 900+ victims feed North Korea's $400 million fake Zoom scheme","description":"North Korean state-sponsored hackers have stolen over $400 million in cryptocurrency during the past 18 months through a sophisticated social engineering operation known as FaceStealer. The attackers compromise legitimate accounts on platforms like Telegram, LinkedIn, Slack, and Discord to invite targets to fake Zoom or Teams meetings. These fraudulent calls display stolen webcam footage of previous victims, creating the illusion of legitimate participants. During the calls, attackers claim audio issues and instruct victims to run malicious scripts that install cookie harvesters, keyloggers, screen capturers, and backdoors. Each successful attack records new victims whose footage is then used in subsequent operations. The scheme primarily targets cryptocurrency wallet holders, web3 workers, and open-source maintainers, with hackers sometimes waiting months or over a year before stealing cryptocurrency funds.","author_name":"AlienVault","modified":"2026-11-13T10:14:28.009000","created":"2026-11-13T09:50:55.466000","revision":2,"tlp":"white","public":1,"adversary":"Lazarus Group","indicators":[{"id":4187584217,"indicator":"uu01webzoom.us","type":"domain","created":"2026-11-13T09:50:56","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null}],"tags":["bluenoroff","social engineering","apt38","fake video calls","north korea","cryptocurrency theft","open-source targeting","facestealer"],"targeted_countries":[],"malware_families":[],"attack_ids":["T1005","T1555.003","T1497","T1113","T1056.002","T1539","T1219","T1059.007","T1078","T1586.002","T1195.002","T1204.002","T1583.001","T1056.001","T1027","T1071.001","T1185","T1534","T1102","T1566.002"],"references":["https:\/\/www.helpnetsecurity.com\/2026\/11\/13\/fbi-north-korean-fake-zoom-calls\/"],"industries":["Technology","Finance"],"extract_source":[],"more_indicators":false},{"id":"6af6dd4e596db4bd8d7a709b","name":"ESXi ransomware: why it is dangerous and how to defend against it","description":"A recent investigation revealed how adversaries systematically destroyed an organization's virtualization estate over ten days by compromising four ESXi hosts, all virtual machines, both vCenter Server Appliances, and backup infrastructure. The attackers, identified as BlackNevas, exploited legitimate VMware management interfaces without using vulnerabilities. They gained access through compromised credentials from non-virtualization systems, established persistence using MeshAgent and Ligolo-ng on vCenter appliances, then leveraged Host Profiles to enable SSH on ESXi hosts. The payload was delivered via SFTP and executed selective overwrites of virtual disk files while backup systems were disrupted first. This demonstrates how hypervisor compromise through valid credentials and administrative features poses severe risk, requiring architectural defenses including treating vCenter as Tier 0 assets, separating virtualization identities from enterprise administration, implementing independent logging infrastr...","author_name":"AlienVault","modified":"2026-11-13T10:07:23.042000","created":"2026-11-13T09:50:06.278000","revision":2,"tlp":"white","public":1,"adversary":"Trial Recovery","indicators":[{"id":2873200793,"indicator":"CVE-2021-21972","type":"CVE","created":"2026-11-13T09:50:07","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":2873968331,"indicator":"CVE-2021-21974","type":"CVE","created":"2026-11-13T09:50:07","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":3009603598,"indicator":"CVE-2021-21985","type":"CVE","created":"2026-11-13T09:50:07","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":3238939511,"indicator":"CVE-2021-22005","type":"CVE","created":"2026-11-13T09:50:07","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":3540283781,"indicator":"188.240.57.94","type":"IPv4","created":"2026-11-13T10:07:11","content":"","title":"","description":"CC=GB ASN=AS25369 hydra communications ltd","expiration":"2026-12-13T10:00:00","is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":3775943829,"indicator":"CVE-2023-34048","type":